.TH FSCK 1
.SH NAME
fsck \- perform file system consistency check
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfsck\fR [\fB\-aciklprSsvz\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fIkb\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIs\fR] [\fB\-L\fR \fIlog\fR] [\fB\-T\fR \fIswapdev\fR] \fIdevice\fR ...
.br
.de FL
.TP
\\fB\\$1\\fR
\\$2
..
.de EX
.TP 20
\\fB\\$1\\fR
# \\$2
..
.SH OPTIONS
.FL "\-a" "Automatically repair inconsistencies"
.FL "\-c" "Check and list only the specified i-nodes"
.FL "\-i" "Report the specified i-nodes when they are found"
.FL "\-k" "Stop when the Back button is pressed (exit status 8, the check is done again next time)"
.FL "\-l" "List the files and directories in the filesytem"
.FL "\-L log" "Append the \fB\-v\fR lines to the file \fIlog\fR"
.FL "\-m kb" "Use at most \fIkb\fR KB for the maps (default 64, at least 8)"
.FL "\-p" "Preen: if the file system is marked clean and not marked for a check, do nothing (one block is read); else check and repair as \fB\-a\fR"
.FL "\-r" "Prompt user for repairs if inconsistencies are found"
.FL "\-S" "The leak sweep: as \fB\-p\fR, but also on a clean file system, and the zone map above the highest zone in use is compared too, so space lost there after a crash is freed. On a mounted file system it only reports"
.FL "\-s" "List the superblock of the file system"
.FL "\-T swapdev" "When the link counts would take more than half of \fB\-m\fR, keep them in the first blocks of the swap partition \fIswapdev\fR (not while swap is on there)"
.FL "\-v" "Short output for the Flipper's 32-column screen: a header, the step with a spinner and a percentage redrawn in place, one line per repair and a summary"
.FL "\-w s" "With \fB\-v\fR: after repairs, keep the summary on the screen until a button or \fIs\fR seconds"
.FL "\-z" "Report the specified zones when they are found"
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX "fsck /dev/hd4" "Check file system on \fI/dev/hd4\fR"
.EX "fsck \-a /dev/hd2" "Automatically fix errors on \fI/dev/hd2\fR"
.EX "fsck \-p \-v /dev/hd1" "What /etc/rc does at boot"
.EX "fsck \-S /dev/hd4" "Free space lost on an unmounted \fI/home\fR"
.EX "fsck \-l /dev/hd4" "List the contents of \fI/dev/hd4\fR"
.EX "fsck \-c 2 3 /dev/hd3" "Check and list \fI/dev/hd3\fR i-nodes 2 & 3"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fIFsck\fR performs consistency checks on the MINIX V2 and V3 file systems which
reside on the specified devices.
When either the \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-p\fR or \fB\-r\fR flags are given, the file
system will be repaired if errors are found.
.PP
Every file system has two flags in its super block (the same as Linux and
util-linux \fIfsck.minix\fR): \fBclean\fR (nothing written since it was
last consistent) and \fBcheck\fR (found unclean, not checked since).
The file system server clears \fBclean\fR before the first write, and sets
it again when nothing has been written for one \fIupdate\fR period, at
\fIumount\fR and at an orderly power off.
\fI/etc/rc\fR runs \fBfsck \-p \-v\fR on the root file system first and on
\fI/usr\fR and \fI/home\fR before mounting them, so only a file system that
was not left clean is checked.
After a check with repairs allowed, \fBcheck\fR is cleared, and \fBclean\fR
is set if the file system is not mounted.
.PP
\fIFsck\fR keeps the computed zone map in memory only for a window of the
zones as large as \fB\-m\fR allows; a larger file system is checked in
several passes (shown as \fIn\fR/\fIN\fR).
The disk's maps are compared one block at a time, and link counts and the
i-node maps are kept for the i-nodes up to the highest one in use.
A window past the first is walked only for the files whose zones reach into
it (two bytes a file: the first and the last window).
Above the highest zone any file uses nothing is walked: without \fB\-p\fR the
map there is compared with the disk; \fB\-p\fR stops at that zone, because
above it the only possible error is space marked used that no file owns (a
loss of space, not a danger); \fB\-S\fR frees that space.
.PP
With very many files (about 10 000 i-nodes in use with \fB\-m 64\fR) the link
counts do not fit in memory.  With \fB\-T\fR \fIswapdev\fR they are kept in
the first blocks of the swap partition, read and written 1 KB at a time
through 16 KB of memory; \fI/etc/rc\fR does this for \fI/usr\fR and
\fI/home\fR (\fB\-T /dev/hd3\fR), before swap is turned on.  Without it such a
check stops with "out of memory" (exit status 8).
.PP
On a MINIX V3 file system (as util-linux \fImkfs.minix \-3\fR makes it, 1 KB
blocks) directory entries are 64 bytes with names of up to 60 characters,
i-node numbers are 32-bit, files may use the triple indirect zone, and the
\fBclean\fR and \fBcheck\fR flags are at byte 14 of the super block (where
MINIX 3.3 keeps its flags; Linux ignores them).
\fIFsck\fR needs about 90 KB of memory: at boot it is free; later, close the
apps first.
.PP
Repairing a mounted file system is safe only for the root file system just
after boot, as \fI/etc/rc\fR does it: if anything was repaired there,
\fIfsck\fR exits with 2 and \fI/etc/rc\fR restarts the system at once.
Any other file system should be unmounted before it is repaired.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 5
0
The file system is clean (or \fB\-p\fR found it marked clean).
.TP 5
1
Errors were found and repaired.
.TP 5
2
The mounted root file system was repaired: reboot now.
.TP 5
4
Errors are left (not repaired).
.TP 5
8
The file system could not be checked (no memory, unreadable, not a V2 or V3 file
system, or stopped with \fB\-k\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.B /etc/rc.conf
\fBfsck_mem\fR: the \fB\-m\fR value used at boot.
.TP 20
.B /usr/adm/fsck.log
What the boot checks of \fI/usr\fR and \fI/home\fR printed.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mkfs (1),
.BR mount (1),
.BR fsck.minix (8)
(util-linux, on a PC).
